{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB09074 - MONDO_16248",
  "verbalization": "Olaparib, a poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitor, is shown to have a clinical relationship with Familial Ovarian Cancer through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Olaparib may exert its therapeutic effect on this condition through the inhibition of cancer cell proliferation, as indicated by its indications for Maligant Granulosa Cell Tumor of Ovary and Immature Ovarian Teratoma, both of which are associated with Familial Ovarian Cancer. These tumors are known to be highly sensitive to platinum-based chemotherapy, such as Cisplatin, which is also linked to Familial Ovarian Cancer. The presence of a direct indication from Olaparib to Cisplatin, and from Cisplatin to Familial Ovarian Cancer, supports the hypothesis that Olaparib may have a role in the treatment of this condition.\n\nThe mechanism of action is further supported by the indirect pathway involving Altretamine, a drug that is linked to Familial Ovarian Cancer. While Altretamine is not directly indicated for Olaparib, the presence of a chain of indications suggests a potential biological link between Olaparib and the disease. The integration of these pathways implies that Olaparib may act through a mechanism that targets cancer cell survival or proliferation, potentially through PARP inhibition, which is a well-established mechanism in ovarian cancer treatment.\n\nThe evidence is consistent with the notion that Olaparib could be a candidate for repurposing in Familial Ovarian Cancer, provided further mechanistic validation is conducted to confirm the direct relationship between Olaparib and the disease. The integration of multiple indications and the presence of a common downstream effect (Cisplatin and Familial Ovarian Cancer) support the hypothesis, but the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
